course: myths
lesson: 33
title: When the teacher gets it wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Teachers know a lot, but they are people, and some of them learned myths at school too: "glass is a liquid", "bats are blind", "lightning never strikes twice".
explain: When you think a teacher is wrong, first check you're right, with a good source. Then ask, don't attack: "I read that spiders are arachnids because of the eight legs, is that right?"
explain: Choose the moment: at the end of the lesson or in private works far better than in front of the whole class. Start by agreeing with what's right, and offer your evidence instead of demanding to win.
explain: If a teacher gets cross, step back and say sorry for the tone; tell a parent calmly at home. And the rules of a place, like a pool's eating rule, still count even when the reason given is a myth.
fact: Chameleons change colour mainly for mood, signalling and temperature, not only to blend in.
fact: Spiders are not insects; they are arachnids with eight legs, while insects have six.
fact: Glass is a solid; some old windows are thicker at the bottom because old glass panes were made uneven.
fact: Warming up with movement is better before running than long still stretches, though stretching won't harm you.
fact: Mount Everest is the highest mountain above sea level; Mauna Kea, a volcano in Hawaii, is taller measured from its base on the sea floor.
fact: There is no good evidence that eating before swimming makes you cramp and drown, but a pool may have its own rules.
fact: Moss grows wherever it is damp and shady, which can be any side of a tree, so it is not a reliable compass.
fact: Lightning often strikes the same place, especially tall things; the Empire State Building, a very tall skyscraper in New York, is hit about 20 times a year.
fact: Bats can see; echolocation adds to their sight.
fact: A good teacher who isn't sure says "I'll check", and thanks the pupil who asked.
fact: A question asked privately, politely and with evidence is far more likely to be welcomed than a public "you're wrong".
fact: If a teacher gets cross, it is wise to step back for now and tell a parent calmly later.
exercise: Why do chameleons mainly change colour? | Mood, signalling and temperature | Only to match their background perfectly | To scare away the rain clouds above | To show everybody what they ate for dinner
exercise: Are spiders insects? | No, they are arachnids with eight legs | Yes, all small creepy crawlies are insects | Yes, but only the big hairy ones | No, they are tiny crabs
exercise: Is glass a liquid? | No, it's a solid; old panes were made uneven | Yes, it slowly flows down the window over centuries | Yes, but only in old churches | No, it's a gas trapped in sand
exercise: What is better before running, says sports science? | A warm-up with movement | Nothing at all, just run | Long still stretches for an hour | Sitting quietly to save energy
exercise: Coach Ryan says "stretch, no arguments". What should you do? | Do the stretches, and suggest moving warm-ups later | Refuse to do any stretches until he admits he's wrong | Walk off the pitch in protest | Tell the team the coach is stupid
exercise: Which mountain is the highest above sea level? | Mount Everest | Mauna Kea | K2 | Kilimanjaro
exercise: Which mountain is the tallest from its base to its top? | Mauna Kea | Mount Everest | Mont Blanc | Ben Nevis
exercise: How could you raise the Mauna Kea point with Miss Parker? | Agree Everest is highest, then ask about Mauna Kea | Shout "Wrong!" as soon as she says Everest | Write "Everest is fake" on the board | Say nothing ever, because teachers are always right
exercise: Mr Shah gets cross: "Don't be cheeky." What's the wise step? | Say sorry for the tone, let it go, tell a parent calmly at home | Argue louder until he admits it | Walk out of the classroom | Get the whole class to back you up
exercise: Mrs Jones says "Good point, I'll check that tonight." What kind of teacher is she? | A great one, and you should thank her for checking | A weak one, who should already know absolutely everything | A lazy one, who just doesn't want to answer the question | A cross one, who is ignoring you because you asked too much
exercise: The swimming instructor says eating before swimming makes you drown. What do you do? | Follow the pool's rule, and tell your parent later | Eat a big lunch and jump in the pool to prove him wrong | Argue with him at the edge of the pool | Never swim again, just in case
exercise: Is moss a reliable compass? | No, it grows where it's damp and shady, on any side | Yes, it always grows on the north side | Yes, but only on oak trees | No, it always grows on the south side, facing the sun
exercise: How could you test the moss tip on a scout hike? | Check mossy trees with a real compass | Follow the moss and see where you end up | Ask the moss which way is north | Count the leaves on every tree
exercise: Does lightning strike the same place twice? | Yes, often, especially tall things | Never, it's a rule of nature | Only once every hundred years | Only if the place is made of metal
exercise: How often is the Empire State Building hit by lightning? | About 20 times a year | Never | Once a century | Every single day
exercise: Mrs Patel says bats are blind. What's the polite correction? | I read they can see too; I can bring the page | Bats aren't blind, you're wrong, everyone knows | Say nothing, bats don't matter | Laugh and tell the class
exercise: Mr Davies says spiders are insects. What's a good way to raise it? | Put your hand up and ask about the eight legs | Shout out that he doesn't know how to do his job | Tell all your friends at lunch that he's useless | Write it on the board when he's not looking at you
test: "sir said glass is liquid, that's why old windows are fat at the bottom?? true?" What's right? | No, glass is solid; old panes were just made uneven | Yes, glass drips very slowly down | Yes, but only in very cold weather | No, glass is really a kind of gas that only looks solid
test: A supply teacher says chameleons change colour "just to hide". When should you ask about it? | At the end of the lesson, as a question | Right away, interrupting her loudly | Never, because supply teachers don't count | In a note left on her desk saying she's wrong
test: Which is the best way to tell a teacher they might be wrong? | Privately, politely, with a good source | Loudly, in front of everyone, to be fair | By getting your parent to shout at them | By refusing to do the work
test: What would happen if you followed moss as a compass in a damp wood? | You could walk the wrong way, since moss grows on any damp side | You'd always go exactly north | You'd always reach a river | The moss would lead you home
test: Mr Grant says the old saying about lightning. Which fact with a number helps most? | The Empire State Building is hit about 20 times a year | Lightning is very bright and hot | Thunder comes after lightning | Most storms happen in summer
test: "Everest is the tallest, end of," says Miss Parker. Which is fairest? | She's right about height above sea level; Mauna Kea is taller from its base | She's totally wrong, Mauna Kea is higher above the sea | She's right and there's nothing more to say | Both are the same height exactly
test: Your teacher snaps "I've been teaching twenty years!" when you correct him. What now? | Say sorry, drop it for now, talk to a parent at home | Tell him twenty years is too long | Keep correcting him in front of the class until he stops | Start crying until he gives in
test: The pool rule says no swimming for an hour after lunch. You know the drowning story is a myth. What do you do? | Follow the rule; places have their own rules | Jump straight in to prove it's just a silly myth | Tell the lifeguard he's an idiot who knows nothing | Complain loudly about it to everyone else at the pool
test: In a quiz cartoon, a spider says "I'm proud to be an insect!" What's the correction? | Spiders are arachnids, with eight legs not six | Spiders are insects, so the cartoon got it right | Spiders are a kind of worm | Spiders are baby crabs
test: Mrs Patel says "Bats are blind, that's why they use echolocation." What's true? | Bats can see; echolocation adds to sight | Bats are completely blind, and deaf as well | Bats only use their eyes, never any echoes | Echolocation is just a word for 'blind seeing'
test: The coach says stretching prevents every injury. What's a fair view? | Moving warm-ups work better, but stretching won't hurt | Stretching causes every injury | Warming up is a total waste of time, just start playing | Only running fast prevents injury
exam: Identify the zoological class to which spiders belong. | Arachnids | Insects | Crustaceans | Reptiles
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the condition of glass at ordinary temperatures? | It is a solid, and unevenness in antique panes arises from their manufacture | It is a liquid that descends perceptibly over centuries | It is a gas held in a rigid form | It is a solid only during the winter months
exam: It is given that a pupil believes a teacher to have stated an error. Select the most appropriate course of action. | To verify the matter and raise it courteously and privately, with evidence | To contradict the teacher forthwith before the entire class | To decline to attend further lessons | To disregard all subsequent instruction from that teacher
exam: Ascertain the approximate annual frequency of lightning strikes upon the Empire State Building. | Approximately twenty | None whatsoever | Approximately one per century | Approximately one thousand
